{"identifier":{"interfaceLanguage":"swift","url":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/Accessibility"},"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/screenstates\/accessibility"]}],"primaryContentSections":[{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Overview","anchor":"Overview"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"A placeholder is often the ","type":"text"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"only"}],"type":"emphasis"},{"text":" thing on screen for a few hundred milliseconds, or the only thing a user with a slow connection sees for much longer than that — so it’s worth it being as accessible as the real content it stands in for. The default ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefault{Empty,Loading,Error}View"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIView"},{"text":"s handle all of this out of the box; this article rounds up what they do and how a fully custom placeholder can match them.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"VoiceOver announcements","anchor":"VoiceOver-announcements"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Each default placeholder announces its own appearance — ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(_:).post()"},{"text":" in SwiftUI, ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.post(notification:.announcement, argument:)"},{"text":" in UIKit — so a screen settling into ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".empty"},{"text":", ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".loading"},{"text":", or ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".error"},{"text":" is heard, not just seen.","type":"text"}]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenState\/accessibilityAnnouncement"},{"text":" exposes that same wording for any case, so a fully custom placeholder can announce transitions consistently instead of inventing its own text:","type":"text"}]},{"syntax":"swift","type":"codeListing","code":[".onAppear {","    if let announcement = state.accessibilityAnnouncement {","        AccessibilityNotification.Announcement(announcement).post()","    }","}"]},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"It’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"nil"},{"text":" for ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".data"},{"text":" — your own content is what VoiceOver should read at that point — and, for ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".empty"},{"text":", it’s the default “Nothing Here” text; announce your own custom title directly instead if your placeholder shows one, so VoiceOver matches what’s on screen. See ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithSwiftUI"},{"text":" and ","type":"text"},{"isActive":true,"type":"reference","identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/GettingStartedWithUIKit"},{"text":" for the full examples.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Reduce Motion","anchor":"Reduce-Motion"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The default Loading placeholder’s ring spins via a repeating rotation animation — purely decorative, and exactly the kind of large, continuous motion Reduce Motion exists to suppress. ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView"},{"text":" reads SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"accessibilityReduceMotion"},{"text":" environment value; ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView"},{"text":" reads ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.isReduceMotionEnabled"},{"text":" and also observes ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.reduceMotionStatusDidChangeNotification"},{"text":", so an already-visible spinner responds immediately if the setting changes while on screen, not just on the next appearance. Either way, the ring stays static — still colorful, just not spinning — instead of disabling itself entirely, since a static ring still communicates “loading” on its own.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Increase Contrast","anchor":"Increase-Contrast"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"All three default placeholders’ gradient icons\/ring respect Increase Contrast — SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"colorSchemeContrast"},{"text":" environment value, UIKit’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIAccessibility.isDarkerSystemColorsEnabled"},{"text":" (observed live the same way Reduce Motion is). A multi-stop gradient can dip below an acceptable contrast ratio in places a single flat color never does, so when Increase Contrast is on, every gradient is swapped for a solid ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".label"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":".primary"},{"text":" tint instead.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Dynamic Type","anchor":"Dynamic-Type"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"The default Empty\/Error placeholders’ text scales with Dynamic Type: SwiftUI’s ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ErrorView"},{"text":" get this for free from ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ContentUnavailableView"},{"text":"’s own text styles, and ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView"},{"text":"\/","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"ErrorUIView"},{"text":"’s labels use ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"UIFont.preferredFont(forTextStyle: .body, compatibleWith:)"},{"text":". Both are covered up to the largest accessibility size by snapshot tests, so a regression that silently pins the text to a fixed size would be caught. The default Loading placeholder has no text at all — its ring is a fixed-size shape, unaffected by Dynamic Type by design.","type":"text"}]},{"level":2,"type":"heading","text":"Identifiers for XCUITest","anchor":"Identifiers-for-XCUITest"},{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"text":"Every default placeholder exposes a stable ","type":"text"},{"type":"codeVoice","code":"accessibilityIdentifier"},{"text":", independent of any localized or custom text, so a UI test can assert which state is showing without string-matching:","type":"text"}]},{"type":"table","rows":[[[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Identifier"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Shown on"}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.loading","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingView","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultLoadingUIView","type":"reference","isActive":true}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.empty","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyView","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultEmptyUIView","type":"reference","isActive":true}],"type":"paragraph"}]],[[{"inlineContent":[{"code":"screenStates.error","type":"codeVoice"}],"type":"paragraph"}],[{"inlineContent":[{"identifier":"doc:\/\/screenstates.ScreenStates\/documentation\/ScreenStates\/ScreenStateDefaultErrorView","type":"reference","isActive":true},{"type":"text","text":"\/"},{"identifier":"doc:\/\/sc